00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.309 align:start position:0%
 
the Lord alone is good evening - I mean  this is a hugely expensive project l  know if you're going to measure whether  it represents value for money to the  taxpayer  well we a desperately short of transport  capacity between our major conurbations  and HS - links london and the west  midlands which are the two largest  conurbations  in the country and then goes north west  to Manchester and northeast to Leeds  which are also hugely important both  economically and in terms of population  conurbations - so there is no free lunch  here if we don't build HS - and a new  rail line to 21st century technology  what we have to do is to modernize the  existing Victorian railway or to be more  precise in the case of London to  Birmingham pre-victorian railway because  the London and Birmingham railway was  opened in 1838 for the coronation of  Queen Victoria and modernizing and  trying to put more capacity longer  platforms changing junctions and all  that on a Victorian rally is hugely  expensive and difficult so if we didn't  do it just - we'd have to spend billions  on upgrades which wouldn't give us more  than a fraction of the benefits what  about this plot this price Oh 55 billion  I mean there are some wild estimates  flying around there was one in one of  the Sunday newspapers that suggested the  ultimate cost could be could be double  that I mean how are we had confidence I  that this project will come in on time  into budget  well you just interviewed Sir David  Higgins is the chairman of HS - Sir  David who was responsible for a large  part of the delivery of the  infrastructure for the Olympic Games is  one of the best infrastructure managers  in the world and the team he's got at HS  - is an excellent team and if anyone  couldn't deliver this this project on  time non-budget it's the team that Sir  David Higgins has put together at HS -  so that should give us reasonable  confidence but of course it's very  important these costs are kept under  control because the big problem not only  in this country but abroad and with  infrastructure projects historically is  that they aren't delivered on time and  on budget and it's very much their job  to see that we get it right this time  quite and that's a big issue though  isn't it I mean who is responsible for  the governance of this there have been  issues raised about the governance of HS  - in the recent past suddenly well the  the company HS - Limited which is a  government company is responsible for  the deliver  we of the project and that couldn't be  clearer it's a very clear allocation of  responsibilities and Sir David Higgins  and his management team hs2 are  responsible but of course it's  absolutely right that the government  keeps a beady eye on on what's going on  and I very much hope that that there is  real attention to cost control because  we do want to see that this railway is  opened the first leg from London to  Birmingham in 2027 which of course is  now only ten years years away so people  can we'll be able to factor it into  their plans whether they're companies  seeking to work out where best to go in  the country or people looking at where  to live so hugely important social and  economic decisions will be taken on the  basis of this railway and it's vitally  important that it's delivered on time  and on budget but this is why people  have concerns isn't it I mean the woes  that we've had at Karelian during the  last seven day show that it is very very  difficult even for the professionals  like Karelian to keep a handle on large  numbers of sprawling projects well there  are fortunately more than one company  that can operate in in this space there  are a large number of international  companies that can and what hs2 has done  is is to contract with a number of them  so that as Sir David Higgins said if  there were to be a problem with Karelian  they would be able to take control of  the contracts and award them elsewhere  but we very much hope that doesn't  happen  now what about Heathrow well we have you  the lot of concerns that the third  runway at Heathrow is going to get  kicked into the long grass again it  didn't get much of a mention in the  Queen's speech and it looks like final  decisions I'm going to be made until  next year now  well they the final decisions can't be  made until next year because there has  to be a process of parliamentary  consultation before Parliament as a  whole is able to to vote on the on the  final government statement of policy  that's required for Heathrow to proceed  but Secretary of State Chris Grayling  has said that he is determined to take  the Heathrow decision forward the  process of parliamentary consultation  has now begun and my commission the  National Infrastructure Commissioner has  said to the government that we expect  that decision to be taken in a timely  way which is next April or May which is  as soon as it can be taken so that the  essentially needed extra capacity  Heathrow can be put in place as soon as  possible  now separately you read  we said that a hard brexit would be the  worst mistake that this country has made  since appeasement in the 1930s was a bit  over-the-top I don't think it is a hard  brexit which would take Britain out of  the central economic institutions of the  European Union that's the customs union  and the single market would imperil our  trade and a very large number of jobs so  I obviously it's a very different  mistake that we'd be making from the  mistake in the 1930s the mistake in the  1930s was essentially a mistake of  defense policy not rearming fast enough  and taking enough notice of what was  going on with a hostile Germany this  would be essentially an act of economic  unilateral disarmament we would be  jeopardizing our trade with Europe for  as most people I think now coming to  realize no good reason at all so it's  essential that in in the jargon that  we're now using that we have what's  called a soft brexit which is that we  stay in the arrangements for the customs  union and a single market so that our  trade and the millions of jobs that  depend upon it can flow just as freely  after brexit as they do now I'll order  Danish we've got to leave it there  appreciate you joining us thank you

